<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100" phx-no-curly-interpolation>
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="connect-dev-containers"
      updated="September 9, 2026"
      evidence="Tested with Dev Containers CLI 0.89.0 on September 7, 2026."
      toc={[
        {"connect", "Connect Dev Containers"},
        {"limits-and-risks", "Limits & risks"},
        {"rebuild", "Rebuild and rotate"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="connect-dev-containers">
        <:dek>
          Run a CLI agent and the emisar bridge inside your existing development container.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Dev Containers are useful when your project already has a containerized toolchain. They
        can isolate the agent from <a
          href={~p"/docs/connect-dev-containers#limits-and-risks"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >most of your host</a>, but the agent can still access anything you mount or forward into
        the container.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          Docker and either the VS Code Dev Containers extension or the standalone Dev Containers
          CLI, with a working
          <.docs_inline_code>.devcontainer</.docs_inline_code>
          setup.
        </:item>
        <:item>Codex installed and signed in inside the container, using a non-root user.</:item>
        <:item>
          An emisar account with permission to connect an agent and at least one runner online.
          Complete the
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >quickstart</.link>
          first if you do not have a runner yet.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_sandbox_connection
        name="Dev Containers"
        connect_href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
        audit_href={~p"/app/audit"}
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="limits-and-risks">Limits &amp; risks</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          The agent can see and potentially leak anything mounted into the container, including
          secrets in
          <.docs_inline_code>.env</.docs_inline_code>
          files and temporary artifacts. Mount only the files it needs. For stronger isolation
          from local files, try <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/connect-coop"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >co:op</.link>.
        </li>
        <li>
          VS Code can share Git credentials or forward an SSH agent into the container. Review its
          <.link
            href="https://code.visualstudio.com/remote/advancedcontainers/sharing-git-credentials"
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >credential-sharing settings</.link>
          before starting the agent, and never mount the host's Docker socket.
        </li>
        <li>
          The default configuration does not restrict outbound network access. We recommend
          allowing access only to the services the agent needs.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="rebuild">Rebuild and rotate</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Keep the bridge's named volume when rebuilding the container so rotated credentials are
        retained. If you remove the volume or the agent's private configuration, create a fresh key from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/agents"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >AI agents</a>
        and reconnect.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
